## 3. Service layer

The controller calls `this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner)` and (many times, via helpers) `this.clinicService.findAllOwners()`.

Interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java` (lines 57, 59).

Impl: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java` — class is `@Service` (line 40).

`saveOwner` (lines 232-237):
```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```

`findAllOwners` (lines 125-129):
```java
@Override
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public Collection<Owner> findAllOwners() throws DataAccessException {
    return ownerRepository.findAll();
}
```

No validation, no exceptions thrown, no side effects in the service layer beyond delegating to the repository. Each method carries its own `@Transactional` (Spring `org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional`); `saveOwner` is a read-write transaction, `findAllOwners` is `readOnly = true`. Because the controller opens no transaction, every `findAllOwners()` helper call and the final `saveOwner()` run in separate transactions.

