## 5. @Valid, custom validators, @ControllerAdvice, @Transactional

- `@Valid` on the request body: `OwnersApi.addOwner` param (line 86). Interface is `@Validated` (line 44).
- No custom `ConstraintValidator` classes exist; all bean validation uses standard Jakarta/Hibernate constraints. The "custom" checks (email regex, telephone E.164 normalization, postcode-region rules, duplicate/identity, per-day and per-city caps) are plain imperative code inside the controller, not validators.
- Exception handling: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` (`@ControllerAdvice`, line 48):
  - `MethodArgumentNotValidException` → 400 with `ProblemDetail` listing field errors (lines 133-154). This handles failures of the `@Valid` bean validation on `OwnerFieldsDto`.
  - `DataIntegrityViolationException` → 404 (lines 113-124) — would fire if a DB constraint is violated at commit.
  - `AccessDeniedException` → rethrown so Spring Security produces 403/entry-point (lines 84-87) — relevant because of `@PreAuthorize`.
  - `Exception` (catch-all) → 500 (lines 96-103). A `ConstraintViolationException` from Hibernate entity validation at flush would generally surface here (500) unless wrapped as a data-integrity violation.
- `@Transactional`: none on the controller method; present on `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` (`@Transactional`, line 233) and `findAllOwners` (`@Transactional(readOnly=true)`, line 126). (`deleteOwner` in the controller has `@Transactional` at line 190, but that is a different endpoint.)

